{:edb/db-before-t 887 :edb/db-after-t 888 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534200 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:39:33.794Z" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128925 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/27/2701e789f5d3f6fc48fda87706add41ccb59c92d5bae4a76e84ac1abfdce6f07.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128925 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/34/34184a95be2b3ae6865fde109ace7e8b4397f4b01982928be73cff4199792d61.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128925 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/b9/b9f56d3f0414f4b56b50e59c2c02d9072f1fb74ce09d801a1ab62abac61ad737.png)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/85/8562a693df46625ee0dc2bcdecc664dfc50a99889fdb855ae2e06dc1db02a3d4.png)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/d2/d263bfe2386309c55f1d63d97d15eaa682e9b0748a9bee6d3378d0b9035927ba.png)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/6c/6ccb00b5e1387cd15847c50d66b26ee903d95428bcff2bfc933b675f9b590717.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128925 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/d8/d8725937a4f5f1cfcd36cbe4871a872187df071b54c15d2e29d9b1e3c5f9b731.svg)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5e/5e3142b1ad4bb219db5d5b144e47fe8d1fa8d1f92c300c6f60fc145cf9424900.svg)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/93/9331fdbede7da49e2dbbc66aac0b3084b1c7acca1ac7e789eded304956a18500.svg)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/76/76e7add6349e0b2fceddf094ac6e3e8cfd0bf1bb43a3fbee0c143b256b81f1c5.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128926 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/56/564d16a59313514424d24b8c247d8a3516bfa32188292d2cbc7fee7faf2f5744.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128926 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/e3/e300a9ac18f0170ae9053c9635fe13a9dd6def793ebfc0bd9dbe81e28810a347.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128926 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/53/53977f4d27cf659f7d595afc4422b0c3009218fe836615eba5faf8a854124c6b.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/40/40fea7849f3603441671253bb7bc96e56be9bab6a8ddcbc94320238a0e3f529a.svg)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/08/08ed315816def9b28fb3918a2dd103a5008815fc6a07507b64c5c20572b1c88f.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128926 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/f3/f3d533c5687da1a75a07c6ad86b40ee8aa6ea5c86cdd250d5c47ff99b885ef61.png)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5d/5d576fca72f6a4f9f0668ff0efbdc2cacc33f8dbb2c00192a7397da9ec83adea.png)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/4f/4f6a034def35760ae94b6eb6a425c59950556a78c124aa9ad0fcfdaa06053b28.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128927 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/08/085cb01a2e4da286ea19a6f1e1707e685646af3d887abb1a57ab50e4d352c53f.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128927 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/aa/aad9e35c3e07e67260a7f3ce96da685be97fab68fe542d262be0ed4f0941f909.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128927 1074 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/d7/d7b93aa8abed895e49b965b8d0905088249475cad98757e7a93a174fae143b49.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128927 1074 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/f8/f89c32a7464b2eeb0f6db6a2c80feef98e8aa24d012b44695e7c7d24b91829ec.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128995 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](images/85/8579b0afd584298a247c38af153d4e69945e73923e9605a168734c9250f3d18a.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128995 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](images/a2/a29e80b35c9505ee665a109978de9b936ee7ce59eef631d7af9a4b7954bd8696.png)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186128995 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](images/11/11af9a5354d23586af399f9bfade0641e9bb10898068ea825755de5c645e3d9c.svg)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186128995 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d2/d2f89fe83787daf667e01fd4c4fe644bc36cfe23b046088c22e8de17bb02dc28.png)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186129113 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413825 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186129114 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413824 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186129115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413822 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186129176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413823 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186129395 1050 420.0 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186130339 1064 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186130339 1064 "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/10/1074870d255109821b5f36eb517f22531efbd8f5069f05c3de3f111b38befd02.svg)\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b1d99269c847f7c6bc0558da347fb300dba5fa6caf373cd1ed5cc5ebacb1649.svg)" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186130380 1064 "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n![](images/af/af61f32297e248ab9989ba59ea6c9bda2f99efe19d25df525fef916617843a32.svg)\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186130380 1064 "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. - All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\nII. - Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." 13194139534200 17592186045417 false] [17592186413798 1080 "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413798 1082 #edb/ref 175921