592186413821 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413822 1069 "q-42373" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413822 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413822 1072 #uuid "17d0a606-b191-57ce-bda5-cb6bdd31b9a8" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413822 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{32}$, ${a}_{43}$ and ${a}_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413820 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1069 "q-129206" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1071 "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1072 #uuid "43229055-b652-5005-bf91-54dcfc3e2941" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413823 1074 "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-6$, $4$, and $1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-6+4+1=-1.$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413818 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1069 "q-42005" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1072 #uuid "72e836b6-2968-5b2e-8666-6c15080ad3e0" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413824 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition  II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since ${b}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is in row echelon form." 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413819 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1069 "q-129230" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -9 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1072 #uuid "a0f43991-a609-584a-a820-e263b283da38" 13194139534200 17592186045417 true] [17592186413825 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -9 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form." 13194139534200 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0bcbaf3a-7d79-5c34-9970-8e30a0164f3d" 17592186413798 "answer-0e03e0f4-f6d8-5e5e-be1b-2065